                                                           🏠 Real Estate Data Analysis Project
📌 Project Overview :
  The Real Estate sector is a significant driver of economic growth and urban development. Understanding price trends, property features,
  and market segments can provide valuable insights for buyers, sellers, and investors. In this project, 
  I analyzed a real estate dataset to uncover key patterns and trends in property listings, focusing on:

Price distributions across cities and property segments

Impact of property features like furnishing, bedrooms, and class

Data cleaning for inconsistent price formats

Visualization of insights for decision-making

The goal was to transform raw real estate data into actionable insights using Excel’s data analysis and visualization tools.

🗂️ Dataset Details
Source: Kaggle 

Total Records: 9000+ property listings

Key Fields:

City: City name of the property

Price: Property price in formats like “2.5 Cr”, “52.5 - 56.7 L”

Bedrooms: Number of bedrooms

Furnishing: Categorical (values like 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 representing furnishing status)

Class: Property classification (A, B, O)

Other property features

🔧 Data Cleaning and Preparation
Price Standardization
One major challenge was inconsistent price formats:

Some values were in crores (Cr), e.g. “2.5 Cr”

Others were in lakhs (L), e.g. “52.5 L”

Some were ranges, e.g. “52.5 - 56.7 L”

Solution:

Created Excel formulas to:

Detect “Cr” or “L” via text search

Extract numeric parts

Convert all prices into a single unit (e.g. INR in Lakhs or Millions)

For ranges, computed the average price

📊 Data Analysis and Insights
Price Distribution
Majority of properties were priced under ₹1 crore

Premium segment (₹2 crore+) concentrated in metro cities

Identified outliers for further investigation

Furnishing Impact
Higher furnishing levels correlated with:

Increased property prices

Shorter average selling durations (where data available)
Bedrooms vs. Pricing
2 BHK and 3 BHK properties were the most listed

Price per square foot increased significantly from 3 BHK to 4 BHK properties

Class Analysis
Class A properties:

Higher price bands

Predominantly luxury or premium listings

Class B:

Mid-market

Class O:

Other or miscellaneous category; varied pricing

📈 Visualizations
Created dynamic visualizations in Excel:

Pivot tables for:

Average price by city

Count of properties by furnishing level

Price distribution across classes

Charts:

Bar charts for price comparisons

Pie charts for furnishing distribution

Line graphs for price trends

Formatted axes to display values in millions for readability, using custom number formats (e.g. #,##0,,"M").

✅ Key Business Takeaways
Properties with premium furnishing attract ~25-30% higher prices.

Metro cities dominate high-value transactions.

Data cleaning significantly improves accuracy of insights and reduces reporting errors.

